# Manuscript PDF page 221

                                THE CHORD



    The river eased. Years of weight on that water slid off by a hair’s
breadth, and Niamh looked at her own hand the way I look at mine at
three in the morning, as if it had just said something unexpected.
    The view swung.
    A wet hill in the dark. A fence post. A woman in muddy boots, with
her jacket zipped wrong at the bottom, beating a plastic lunch-box lid
against the wood in a rhythm that didn’t seem to have a name. Ruth.
She’d come straight from her shift at the grounds depot, and a col‐
league called Mo stood behind her holding her bag and looking at the
sky as if it owed him a result.
    Stonehenge was a mile off. She wasn’t near it. She stood in a farmer’s
field, on a patch of ground she had every right to be on, hitting a Tup‐
perware lid with something close to joy.
    Ruth’s answer was a rest. Strike, then space. Strike, then space. A
hollow in the sound where somebody else could step in without hav‐
ing to match her. When I reached for it I nearly fell, because the space
was generous. For months I had been crushing exactly that hollow, the
open room I couldn’t stand to leave unfilled.
    Ruth moved one boot because the ground was uneven.
    The connection held.
    No heroics in it at all. A woman accommodating a slope.



Giza came in warm.
    A flat roof, a water tank, a plastic chair, a line of laundry stirring. Be‐
hind the apartment blocks, vast and dark and almost ordinary against
the stars, the pyramids. A schoolteacher named Salma Hassan sat on
the edge of the roof with a copper bowl on her knee that had belonged
to her aunt. Her neighbor Mariam sat by the stair door with a thermos,
pretending not to be frightened.
    Salma was not inside any chamber. She had gotten up before dawn,
looked in on her mother, and gone up the stairs.
    She rapped the bowl with a knuckle. It gave out a note with a burr in
it, a rough, buzzing edge, like a bee in a jar.




